    "body": "## Investigation Results\n\n**Status**: MEDIUM confidence \u2014 plausible \"Environment timing\" root cause in a genuine timer-based renewal path, but this specific test structurally cannot be redesigned around the timing it verifies, and no CI rerun was available to confirm.\n\n### Reproduction attempt\n\nSame session-wide limitation noted on the other issues in this batch: `gh run rerun`/the REST equivalent returns `403 Resource not accessible by integration` \u2014 no confirmation rerun was possible. Falling back to code analysis.\n\n### Root cause analysis\n\nThe test exercises `G2GTransferPusherService`'s periodic transfer-key keep-alive reminder, configured here with `KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_MS = 100`. It waits 200ms (2 nominal intervals) and reads the key's `expireAt`, then waits another 300ms (3 more nominal intervals) and reads it again, asserting each read is strictly later than the previous:\n\n```ts\nawait delay(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_MS * 2);\nconst earlyInExport = await readExpireAt(tk.key);\nawait delay(KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_MS * 3);\nconst laterInExport = await readExpireAt(tk.key);\n...\nexpect(earlyInExport).toBeGreaterThan(before);\nexpect(laterInExport).toBeGreaterThan(earlyInExport);   // <- failed here: both equal 1787835387290\n```\n\nBoth margins are nominally generous (2\u20133 keep-alive ticks expected per window), but under heavy CI resource contention \u2014 the same class of event-loop/scheduling pressure already implicated in the `beforeAll` hook timeouts investigated alongside this issue (#11820\u2013#11822) \u2014 a 100ms `setInterval`-based reminder can occasionally be delayed enough that zero ticks land inside one of these windows, producing two reads of the *same* underlying `expireAt` value (an exact tie, not a near-miss).\n\nThis is a real production code path (the keep-alive reminder) under test, not a test-only artifact \u2014 but unlike the `getInstance()` hook timeouts, this one is harder to fix with a bare margin increase alone: the boundary condition **is** the thing under test (a real timer firing), so there's no \"seed the state directly\" shortcut available (the Step 3 guardrail's redesign advice doesn't have a clean target here \u2014 you can't skip to the boundary without testing something else). A larger `KEEP_ALIVE_INTERVAL_MS` / longer delay windows would make the flake statistically rarer but not deterministic, and changing the second assertion to `toBeGreaterThanOrEqual` would hide a real \"the reminder stalled for a whole window\" regression, not just tolerate a false alarm.\n\n### Why this isn't HIGH confidence\n\n- Could not distinguish with certainty between \"genuine event-loop scheduling jitter under CI load\" (a timing margin problem, matching #11820-#11822's pattern) and a subtler off-by-one in how the reminder's own interval is scheduled/restarted (a real product bug) \u2014 both produce the same symptom (a tied read), and I could not empirically test either hypothesis without a CI rerun.\n- No safe redesign path was found that both removes the scaling/timing sensitivity and still tests what this test is for (a real periodic timer's renewal behavior).\n\n### Recommendation\n\n1. A human familiar with `G2GTransferPusherService`'s keep-alive implementation should check whether the reminder's `setInterval` (or equivalent) is re-armed correctly on every tick, or whether it can silently stop early under certain conditions \u2014 that would turn this from a timing flake into a real product bug.\n2. If the timer implementation is confirmed correct, widen this test's delay windows (e.g. 2/3 nominal intervals \u2192 5/8) to reduce false-positive probability under CI load, while keeping the strict `toBeGreaterThan` assertions (a margin increase here is the legitimate exception to the Step 3 guardrail, since \u2014 unlike an N-round-trip loop \u2014 there is no way to skip straight to the boundary without testing something else).\n\nNo code change made in this issue \u2014 flagging for human review per the MEDIUM-confidence gate.\n\n---\n*Investigated by Claude Code (autonomous flaky-ci-routine)*\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
